## Invariants

- New runtime schema must update `001_schema.sql`; do not rely only on an
  incremental migration for fresh installs.
- Migration filenames must use the next unused numeric prefix; avoid duplicate
  prefixes because files sort lexically.
- Column removal or data reshaping belongs in an incremental migration with
  guarded reads/writes.
- Auth secrets stay in auth tables, not `users`.
- `001_schema.sql` contains the current inbox-participant and provisioning
  baseline before `002_seed.sql`; migrations 018 and 029 remain guarded bridges
  for older deployments.
- Migration 035 fails closed when normalized response rows or palette tokens
  exist, then drops disposable shadow, telemetry, theme, section, and response
  tables. `pages.content` remains the sole custom-page authority.
- Migration 036 is the idempotent existing-tenant bridge for `documentations`,
  `documentation_sections`, and `documentation_articles`; fresh installs receive
  the same schema and `docs.create`/`docs.manage` grants from 001/002.
- Migration 034 is additive/idempotent and runs after guarded historical cleanup.
  Its delete triggers deliberately install last so migration-only `DELETE`
  statements remain classified separately from forbidden runtime SQL.
- The hard-delete bypass is the global NOLOGIN
  `skaia_hard_delete_operator` role. Migrations create but never grant it;
  operator use requires an explicit reviewed grant and revocation.
- Migration 040 and the fresh schema define `service_incidents` and
  `service_incident_events`; incident event hard deletion uses the shared guard.

## Verify

- `find backend/internal/migrations -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.sql' | sort`
- `rg "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS|ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS|ON CONFLICT" backend/internal/migrations`

